mon property. Set notation is a way of representing sets using symbols and mathematical notation.\n\nHere are some basics of set notation:\n\n1. Set notation is often denoted using braces: { }, which contain the elements of the set separated by commas. For example, the set of natural numbers less than 5 can be written as {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}.\n\n2. Sometimes, we use ellipses to represent a pattern or sequence of elements. For example, the set of even natural numbers can be written as {2, 4, 6, ...}.\n\n3. If an element is in a set, we use the symbol "∈" to show that it belongs to that set. For example, 3 ∈ {1, 2, 3, 4}.\n\n4. If an element is not in a set, we use the symbol "∉" to show that it does not belong to that set. For example, 5 ∉ {1, 2, 3, 4}.\n\n5. We can use set-builder notation to define a set by specifying a property that its elements share. For example, the set of even numbers can be written as {x : x is an integer and x is even}.\n\n6. We use mathematical symbols to denote the relationship between sets. For example, the symbol "⊂" (subset) is used to show that one set is a subset of another. For example, {1, 2, 3} ⊂ {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}.\n\nI hope this helps!\nuser: I need to retrieve the seasons for team statistics for the teams with IDs 7, 10, and 15.\nassistant: Fetching season data for teams 7, 10, and 15 now.\nuser: Can you also get the average points per season for each of these teams?\nassistant: Retrieving average points per season for teams 7, 10, and 15.\nuser: Great. Now compile all this into a summary: team ID, seasons played, and average points.\nassistant: Compiling a summary for teams 7, 10, and 15 with their seasons and average points...\nuser: Can you find me some funny jokes related to computers? I\'d prefer short, one-liner types without any offensive content.\nassistant: Absolutely! Let me search for some clean, one-liner computer jokes for you.\nassistant: Here are three clean, one-liner computer jokes for you:\n\n1. Why do programmers prefer dark mode? Because light attracts bugs.\n2. I told my computer I needed a break, and now it won’t stop sending me vacation ads.\n3. Why was the computer cold? It left its Windows open.\n\nHope these gave you a chuckle! 😊\nuser: Hey, I\'m on the hunt for some real estate gold. Can you pull up the active listings from agent ID ZXY123? Let\'s see what treasures they have for us today!\nassistant: Absolutely! Let me fetch the active listings for agent ZXY123 right away.\nassistant: The agent with ID "ZXY123" has 2 active listings:\n\n1. The first listing is located at 123 Main St, San Francisco, CA, 94101. The price for this listing is $2,400,000.\n2. The second listing is located at 456 Elm St, San Francisco, CA, 94101. This one is priced at $2,800,000. \n\nPlease let me know if there\'s anything else I can assist you with.\nuser: Wow, those prices are sky-high! Looks like the real estate market is really booming in San Francisco.\nassistant: Indeed, San Francisco is known for its strong and competitive real estate market. The high prices reflect the demand and the value properties hold in that region. If you\'re interested in more detailed information or in other properties, feel free to let me know how I can assist further!\nuser: True that, assistant. Anyway, can you also find me what the orderbook depth is for the NFT collection "Golden_Rush" on OpenSea? I\'m simultaneously dabbling in digital assets.\nassistant: Got it — let me pull up the orderbook depth for the \'Golden_Rush\' NFT collection on OpenSea right away.\nuser: With the increase in remote work, I\'m finding it hard to stay connected with my team. I\'d like to set up regular interactions with my coworkers using different formats based on our work schedules. I prefer video calls in the early morning or audio calls in the afternoon, each lasting about 30 minutes. Also, could you optimize my work schedule if I prefer to start early in the morning and work no more than 6 hours a day?\nassistant: I can help you optimize your work schedule based on your preferences and suggest a structured rhythm for team interactions. Let me first analyze your ideal work hours.\nassistant: Great! Based on your preference to start early and work no more than 6 hours, I\'ve optimized your schedule as follows:\n\n- **Work Hours**: 7:00 AM – 1:00 PM (with a 15-minute break at 9:30 AM)\n- **Team Interactions**:\n  - 🎥 Video Call: 7:30 AM (30 min) — perfect for early morning connection\n  - 🎧 Audio Call: 1:00 PM (30 min) — ideal wind-down chat after focused work\n\nThis structure keeps your day productive, limits burnout, and ensures meaningful, time-efficient interactions with your team. Would you like me to generate calendar invites or reminders for these sessions?<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
