en-Scott South Pole Station at the South Pole. The journey from the South Pole to the coast takes about ten days using tracked vehicles.\n2nd stage: From the Antarctic coast to the North Pole by ship\nThe geographic North Pole lies on the North American Plate, but not on land, but on a 2 to 3 m thick floating ice sheet. Below lies the Arctic Ocean, which is 4087 m deep at this point. The North Pole can be reached both by heavy icebreakers and by submarine.\nSubmarine:\nA submarine trip from McMurdo Station on the Antarctic coast to the North Pole is possible. The route to the North Pole would roughly follow the date line, east past New Zealand through the Pacific Ocean to the Bering Strait. After passing through the Bering Strait, the submarine reaches the Arctic Ocean and there, under the ice, the North Pole. The distance covered is approximately 18,255 km. The maximum speed of modern submarines is around 75 km/h. The route to the North Pole would therefore take approximately 245 hours, or 10 days and 5 hours.\nIcebreaker:\nTo get from McMurdo Station to the North Pole by ship without changing trains, you need an icebreaker. The maximum speed of an icebreaker, such as the Russian nuclear icebreaker \'Pobjeda\', is approximately 40 km/h. The route to the North Pole would therefore take approximately 456 hours or 19 days.\n\nThe fastest way to get from the South Pole to the North Pole would be to travel approximately 10 days from the South Pole to the coast in a tracked vehicle, followed by another 10 days in a submarine. It takes at least 40 days to travel from the South Pole to the North Pole and back.\nuser: Role definition:\n Inquirer: A user who raises an inquiry.\n Response assistant: Communicates with the inquirer and provides answers and solutions.\n\n Historical dialog data is as follows:\nInquirer: Can you analyze the personality traits from my speech during the team meeting about teamwork and from my presentation yesterday focusing on leadership?\nResponse