INFO 09-21 18:28:07 logger.py:37] Received request chatcmpl-ca41ba3bd5104e29bce329fb1deb5c89: prompt: '<|im_start|>system\nYou are building a long-term memory for an AI assistant. From the dialogue excerpt, extract atomic fact memories as a JSON object: {"memories": ["<single declarative sentence>", ...]}. Keep identifiers, addresses, URLs and configuration values VERBATIM. One fact per entry; include the user preference, attribute or event each entry refers to. Output JSON only.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nDialogue excerpt:\nuser: Can you explain what glycogen and starch are, and why fungi and plants use different forms for storage?\nassistant: Sure! \n\nGlycogen and starch are both types of polysaccharides, which are long chains of sugar molecules. Glycogen is the form of glucose storage in animals, while starch is the form of glucose storage in plants.\n\nFungi, on the other hand, can use both forms of glucose storage depending on the species. Some fungi store glucose as glycogen, while others store it as starch.\n\nThe reason why plants and fungi use different forms of glucose storage likely has to do with their evolutionary histories and the specific needs of their respective lifestyles. Plants are sessile organisms that rely on starch as a long-term energy reserve to support growth and reproduction. Fungi, on the other hand, are heterotrophic organisms that obtain nutrients from a variety of sources, including dead organic matter. This flexibility may allow them to use either glycogen or starch depending on their environmental conditions and nutrient availability.\n\nOverall, the different forms of glucose storage used by fungi and plants likely reflect adaptations to their unique lifestyles and nutritional requirements.\nuser: I have four different paragraphs that I need proofread. Here they are: "It\'s is a common mistake to miss. There are alot of errors that can sneak in your writing." "their going to face challenges with they\'re new software." "Effectively communication is key to sucessful project management." "We can ensure that every employees has access to the required resources."\nassistant: I can help you proofread these paragraphs for grammar, spelling, and clarity. Let me analyze each one carefully.\nuser: Great! Please correct them and explain the fixes so I can learn from them.\nassistant: Of course! I\'ll correct each paragraph and provide clear explanations for the changes. Here we go:\n\n1. Original: "It\'s is a common mistake to miss. There are alot of errors that can sneak in your writing."\n   Corrected: "It\'s a common mistake to miss errors. There are a lot of errors that can sneak into your writing."\n   Fixes: \n      - Removed redundant "is" after "It\'s" ("It\'s" already means "It is").\n      - Added "errors" for clarity.\n      - Corrected "alot" to "a lot" (it\'s two words).\n      - Changed "sneak in" to "sneak into" for proper preposition use.\n\n2. Original: "their going to face challenges with they\'re new software."\n   Corrected: "They\'re going to face challenges with their new software."\n   Fixes:\n      - "their" → "They\'re" (contraction of "They are")\n      - "they\'re" → "their" (possessive form)\n\n3. Original: "Effectively communication is key to sucessful project management."\n   Corrected: "Effective communication is key to successful project management."\n   Fixes:\n      - "Effectively" (adverb) → "Effective" (adjective) to modify "communication"\n      - Corrected spelling of "sucessful" to "successful"\n\n4. Original: "We can ensure that every employees has access to the required resources."\n   Corrected: "We can ensure that every employee has access to the required resources."\n   Fixes:\n      - "employees" (plural) → "employee" (singular) because "every" requires singular noun\n\nLet me know if you\'d like me to check another draft or explain any part in more detail!<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_pe