ill reflect on the \nstrategies they used during the game and explore mathematical concepts such as \ncounting and adding stones.\n6. Concluding activity: The students will present their reflections on the game \nto the class, explaining their strategies and the mathematical concepts they \nlearned.\n\nAssessment:\n\n- Observing the students during the game and their reflections after the game.\n- Encouraging the students to ask questions and discuss their strategies with \ntheir classmates.\n\nConclusion:\nPlaying Mancala/Oware is an excellent way to develop numeracy and strategic thinking skills among primary school children. The game helps students to understand mathematical concepts such as counting and adding while also developing their strategic thinking skills by making decisions on how to move stones. The history and wide geographic spread of the game make it an excellent tool for exploring cultural significance and promoting cultural awareness.\nuser: Please write a 5 point proposal to the school board for including this lesson plan in the curriculum.\nassistant: Enhancing Mathematical and Strategic Skills: The Mancala/Oware lesson plan\nhas been designed to help primary school children develop their numeracy and\nstrategic thinking skills. This will provide students with hands-on experience\nand help them apply mathematical concepts in a fun and engaging way.\n\nPromoting Cultural Awareness: The lesson plan includes a historical background\non the game, emphasizing its origin and wide geographic spread. This will help\nstudents understand the cultural significance of the game in different parts of\nthe world and promote cultural awareness.\n\nGame-based Learning: The Mancala/Oware lesson plan has been designed to\npromote game-based learning, which is a fun and engaging way to learn. Students\nwill have an opportunity to play the game and apply the mathematical and\nstrategic concepts they have learned.\n\nEnhancing Communication Skills: The reflection and