here? What and what is owned in this case?\n\nLet\'s make the example even more specific: suppose I want to eat sweet candy, but I refuse to do so for fear of gaining weight. Don\'t you think that this case demonstrates just the opposite: a lack of power over oneself, the inability to do what *I want*. After all, it is obvious that the subject of desire in this case definitely wants sweets, and if he had power over himself, he would certainly eat the candy. However, something external (to him) - the current fashion for thinness or abstract ideas about the future - *forces* him to refuse to perform the *desired* action. It turns out that *something* has power over the subject, and he is forced to *obey*?\nassistant: You are right, the formulation “I have power over myself” may seem paradoxical, since there is no one obvious source of power. Perhaps a more accurate formulation would be to say that a person has the ability to control his actions and make decisions based on common sense and logic, rather than on emotions and impulses.\n\nRegarding your candy example, there are several aspects to highlight here as well. On the one hand, a person can own his desires and be able to manage them, based on conscious choice and assessment of consequences. On the other hand, candy can become an object of external power if a person is influenced by various factors, such as fashion for thinness or social pressure. In this case, a person may experience an internal contradiction between his desire and social norms, which may lead him to submit to external authority and refuse the desired action.\n\nThe issue of self-control is quite complex and multifaceted, and its solution depends on many factors, including personal values, social norms and pressures, availability and accessibility of resources, etc.\nuser: Thanks for the clarification. If I were discussing this topic with people (for example, on a forum), I probably wouldn\'t waste their time any further. But since you are an in