s sense, to exercise “power over oneself,” the prefrontal cortex must prevail over other areas of the brain, including the limbic system, which is responsible for emotions. And the examples of “crazy” actions you gave are an example of the absence of such “power”. Thus, although the emotional sphere is an important component of humanity, ideally it should be “under the rule” of reason. Another thing is that such an ideal is unattainable for ordinary people. However, some representatives of spiritual practices, such as the Dalai Lama, can demonstrate some success in this area.\nuser: Can you provide the latest GDP data for Japan?\nassistant: Fetching the latest GDP data for Japan now.\nassistant: Here is the latest GDP data for Japan:\n\n- **GDP (2023)**: $4.23 trillion USD\n- **ISO Code**: JP\n- **Source**: World Bank\n\nLet me know if you\'d like data for another country or a historical trend!\nuser: Hey, I\'m having trouble finding a specific email attachment. Can you help me sort through them? The attachment was sent sometime last month, and it\'s a PDF.\nassistant: Of course! Let me search for PDF attachments from last month for you.\nassistant: Based on the list retrieved from the "getAttachments" tool, here are the PDF attachments sent to you last month:\n\n1. Attachment named "Financial_Report_2024.pdf" was created on 2024-10-01 at 12:34:56 UTC. The unique identifier for this attachment is "attachment-123".\n   \n2. Attachment named "Project_Outline_2024.pdf" was created on 2024-09-30 at 09:21:43 UTC. The unique identifier for this attachment is "attachment-456".\n\nYou can look for these two attachments in your email.\nuser: Thanks for that. This was really helpful. Is there any advice you can give me on how to better organize my email attachments? I feel like I\'m losing a lot of precious time searching through files.\nassistant: Indeed, organizing your email attachments can save you a significant amount of time. Here are a few tips you might find helpful:\