else}\n  <ul>\n    {#each data.Page.media as anime}\n      <li>\n        <h2>{anime.title.english || anime.title.romaji}</h2>\n        <p>Rating: {anime.averageScore}</p>\n      </li>\n    {/each}\n  </ul>\n{/if}\n```\n6. Style the component using Material UI:\n```\n<slot></slot>\n\n<style>\n  :global(body) {\n    margin: 0;\n    font-family: "Roboto", sans-serif;\n  }\n  ul {\n    list-style: none;\n    padding: 0;\n  }\n  li {\n    padding: 16px;\n    border-bottom: 1px solid #eee;\n  }\n  h2 {\n    margin: 0;\n    font-size: 18px;\n    font-weight: 500;\n    color: #333;\n  }\n  p {\n    margin: 0;\n    font-size: 14px;\n    font-weight: 400;\n    color: #666;\n  }\n</style>\n```\n7. Render the component in your main app component:\n```\n<template>\n  <div class="container">\n    <h1>Anime Titles and Ratings</h1>\n    <AnimeList />\n  </div>\n</template>\n\n<script>\n  import AnimeList from "./AnimeList.svelte";\n</script>\n\n<style>\n  .container {\n    display: flex;\n    flex-direction: column;\n    align-items: center;\n    padding: 32px;\n  }\n  h1 {\n    margin-bottom: 16px;\n    font-size: 24px;\n    font-weight: 500;\n    color: #333;\n  }\n</style>\n```\nThat\'s it! You should now have a Svelte app that fetches anime titles and ratings from AniList using GraphQL and displays them using Material UI components.\nuser: Thank you! Exactly what I needed, Can you add some more complications?\nassistant: Sure, I can suggest a few ways to make this app more complicated and challenging:\n\n1. Add pagination: By default, the AniList API returns only 10 results per page. You can add pagination to your app so that users can view more results.\n\n2. Add search functionality: Allow users to search for specific anime titles by adding a search bar and updating the GraphQL query to filter results based on search terms.\n\n3. Add user authentication: If you want to create a more robust app, you can add user authentication using a third-party service like Auth0 or Firebase