WAV files...\nuser: Great! Now create a master mix by combining both tracks with a limiter set to -1 dB ceiling and export it as \'ep_master.wav\'.\nassistant: Combining tracks into a master mix with limiter set to -1 dB ceiling...\nuser: How to do operations with Bytes in JavaScript\nassistant: JavaScript does not have a specific data type for representing bytes, but you can work with them using integers and perform basic arithmetic operations on them.\n\nHere are some common operations you can perform with bytes in JavaScript:\n\n1. Convert to number: To convert a number to a byte, you can perform a bitmask operation using the "&" operator and the number 255 (which is the maximum value that a byte can contain). For example:\n\nlet num = 300;\nlet byte = num & 255;\nconsole.log(byte); // 44\n\n2. Addition and subtraction: You can add and subtract bytes with integers and store the result in an integer variable.\n\nlet a = 200;\nlet b = 100;\nlet c = a + b;\nconsole.log(c); // 300\n\n3. Bit Shift: You can shift the bits of a byte to the left or right using the bit shift operators "<<" and ">>". For example:\n\nlet byte = 5;\nlet leftShift = byte << 2;\nconsole.log(leftShift); // 20\n\nlet rightShift = byte >> 2;\nconsole.log(rightShift); // 1\n\nThese are some basic examples of byte operations in JavaScript. I hope you find them useful.\nuser: Can you provide me the current nitrous oxide levels in the atmosphere?\nassistant: I cannot provide the current nitrous oxide levels in the atmosphere because there are no functions available to retrieve this information.\nuser: Why is the sky orange during sunsets?\nassistant: The sky is orange during sunsets because of the refraction of sunlight through the Earth\'s atmosphere. Light travels through the atmosphere and passes through more and more particles, scattering it in all directions and coloring it red, orange, and yellow. This is why the sky is often more colorful at the horizon and becomes lighter the further you look