INFO 09-21 18:31:18 logger.py:37] Received request chatcmpl-1840fe77ece34476a46ed796d465e95b: prompt: '<|im_start|>system\nYou are building a long-term memory for an AI assistant. From the dialogue excerpt, extract atomic fact memories as a JSON object: {"memories": ["<single declarative sentence>", ...]}. Keep identifiers, addresses, URLs and configuration values VERBATIM. One fact per entry; include the user preference, attribute or event each entry refers to. Output JSON only.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\nDialogue excerpt:\nassistant: Sure, I\'d be happy to explain!\n\nA moving average is a statistical calculation used to analyze data points by creating a series of averages of different subsets of the full data set. In stock market analysis, it is often used to analyze the price of a stock over time.\n\nThere are several types of moving averages, but the most common ones are the simple moving average (SMA) and the exponential moving average (EMA). The SMA takes the average of a specific number of periods, while the EMA gives more weight to recent prices.\n\nHere\'s how it works: Let\'s say we want to calculate the 5-day moving average of a certain stock. For the simple moving average, we\'d add up the closing prices of the stock for the past 5 days and divide by 5. The result is the average closing price over the 5 day period - this is your 5-day SMA.\n\nMoving averages are commonly used in technical analysis to:\n1. Smooth out price data to identify trends: By plotting a moving average, the random price movements are smoothed out, which helps traders identify the underlying trend.\n2. Generate buy and sell signals: When the price crosses above the moving average, it generates a potential buy signal. A sell signal is triggered when the price falls below the moving average.\n\nRemember, while moving averages can help suggest the direction of a trend, they cannot predict future price movements. They should be used with other indicators to provide more confirmation 