nsurance info? My email is familyhelp@example.com.\nassistant: I\'ll compile a summary with all the details and send it to familyhelp@example.com right away.\nassistant: I\'ve sent a detailed summary with addresses, phone numbers, availability, and insurance information to familyhelp@example.com. Both counselors are highly recommended, and one is available as soon as tomorrow. Please reach out when you\'re ready — you\'re taking an important step toward healing.\nuser: Why did Void Linux return to OpenSSL?\nassistant: Hello! The developers of the Void Linux distribution have decided to return to using the OpenSSL library instead of LibreSSL from March 5, 2021. The main reasons for this transition are as follows:\n- Most programs target OpenSSL, so Void will no longer have to maintain (in some cases very complex) patches for compatibility with LibreSSL.\n- The complexity of the OpenSSL API makes such patching time-consuming and risky, with possible errors that can cause problems with the operation or security of programs.\n- Extensive support for *platform-specific optimizations* (that is, optimizations that take into account the characteristics of a specific platform and use them to improve performance) beyond the x86 architecture.\n- Access to new standards and algorithms earlier, for example, full support for the TLS 1.3 protocol.\n\nIt\'s also worth noting that most other distributions that used LibreSSL have abandoned it, so there aren\'t many people to split the development workload between. For example, the Alpine Linux distribution, which was temporarily switching to LibreSSL, returned to OpenSSL in January 2019.\n\nDo you want to learn more about the OpenSSL and LibreSSL libraries?\nuser: Yes, come on\nassistant: OpenSSL is a full-fledged open source cryptography library, widely known for its SSL/TLS extension used in the HTTPS web protocol.\nSupports almost all low-level hashing, encryption and electronic signature algorithms, and also implements most popular cryptographic standards, including the ability to create RSA, DH, DSA keys, X.509 certificates, sign them, generate CSR and CRT, encrypt data and test SSL/TLS connections.\nAvailable as packages for most UNIX-like operating systems (including Solaris/OpenSolaris, Linux, macOS, QNX4, QNX6 and four open source BSD operating systems), as well as OpenVMS and Microsoft Windows.\nOpenSSL is based on SSLeay, written by Eric A. Young and Tim Hudson, who unofficially finished working on it in December 1998 when they began working on the RSA Security project.\n\nLibreSSL is a library with an implementation of the SSL/TLS protocols. This library is a product of the OpenBSD team, within which a fork of OpenSSL is being developed, aimed at providing the highest level of security. Before the fork was founded, the heartbleed vulnerability in OpenSSL was discovered.\nAmong the features of LibreSSL, one can note its focus on high-quality support for the SSL/TLS protocols with the removal of unnecessary functionality, the use of additional security tools and significant cleaning and processing of the code base.\nThe OpenBSD project is developing a native edition of the LibreSSL package for OpenBSD itself and a portable edition for Unix-like systems and Microsoft Windows.<|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>assistant\n', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.0, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=[], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=2000, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: None, lora_request: None, prompt_adapter_request: None.
