achines are a theoretical construct and do not exist in physical reality. A real-world algorithm would never have access to an oracle that can solve problems instantly, so the time complexity gained when using an oracle machine is not directly applicable to real-world computing.\nuser: How about quantum computers? Can\'t they, at least in some cases, be seen as the oracle?\nassistant: Not necessarily. Although potentially faster than classical computers, quantum computers still have a non-zero time complexity and can only solve the problems that can also be computed with a classical computer. In other words, quantum computers adhere to the Church–Turing thesis: they cannot compute more things than classical computers, they can only compute them faster.\n\nThe oracle is a theoretical construct that can compute any problem in a single step, including ones like the halting problem (whether a program will stop eventually or run forever). The halting problem is impossible to compute using classical computers. Since the halting problem is impossible to compute with a classical computer, a quantum computer would be bound by the same limitation. A computer that cannot compute the halting problem in a single step is not considered an oracle, therefore quantum computers cannot be oracles.\nuser: I need thumbnails extracted from three different videos. The details are as follows:\n1. Video path: /videos/video1.mp4, Timestamp: 00:02:15\n2. Video path: /videos/video2.mp4, Timestamp: 00:05:30\n3. Video path: /videos/video3.mp4, Timestamp: 00:01:45\nassistant: Got it — extracting thumbnails from all three videos at the specified timestamps now.\nuser: Great! Can you now rename the thumbnails to include the video name and timestamp, like video1_00-02-15.jpg?\nassistant: Renaming thumbnails with video name and timestamp format now.\nuser: Perfect. Now list all the renamed thumbnails in the /thumbnails directory and confirm they’re all there.\nassistant: Listing all renamed thumbnail