ement. It is calculated by comparing the current high to the previous high.\n\n2. **-DI (Negative Directional Indicator)**: This measures the strength of downward price movement. It is calculated by comparing the current low to the previous low.\n\n3. **ADX (Average Directional Index)**: This measures the overall strength of the trend, regardless of its direction. It is derived from the +DI and -DI values.\n\n### How the DMI Works:\n- The +DI and -DI values indicate the direction and magnitude of price movement over a given period. \n- The ADX value indicates the strength of the trend. An ADX value above 20 or 25 typically signifies a strong trend, while a value below 20 indicates a weak trend or a range-bound market.\n\n### Interpretation:\n- **+DI above -DI**: This indicates a bullish trend, suggesting that upward price movements are stronger.\n- **-DI above +DI**: This indicates a bearish trend, suggesting that downward price movements are stronger.\n- **Rising ADX**: This suggests that the strength of the trend is increasing.\n- **Falling ADX**: This suggests that the strength of the trend is decreasing.\n\n### Example:\nIf the +DI is higher than the -DI and the ADX is rising, it indicates a strong bullish trend. Conversely, if the -DI is higher than the +DI and the ADX is rising, it indicates a strong bearish trend.\n\n### Practical Use:\nTraders use the DMI to make informed decisions about entering or exiting trades based on the direction and strength of market trends. It helps them to avoid trading in weak trends and to identify strong trends where profits can be maximized.\nuser: Well, after understanding the Directional Movement Index, I think it\'s way beneficial to leverage such insights while investing in cryptocurrencies. Speaking of which, I\'ve actually been toying with the idea of doubling down on my Bitcoin investments recently.\nassistant: That sounds like an exciting venture! Doubling down on your Bitcoin investment could potentially be profitable